<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> true all-inclusive ski resorts, where one price covers lodging, lift tickets, lessons, meals, and drinks, are rare in North America. Club Med Québec Charlevoix is the clearest example. A few luxury ranches in Montana bundle skiing into their stays, and many lodges and tour operators sell packages that cover part of the trip. Always check exactly what's included, especially rentals, lesson days, and lift-ticket days.</p></div>

<h2>What "all-inclusive" means for a ski trip</h2>
<p>At a beach resort, all-inclusive usually means your room, food, and drinks. A ski trip has more moving parts, so the label covers a wider range of deals. In practice you'll see three kinds:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Fully inclusive resorts</strong> that bundle lodging, lift access, group lessons, meals, and drinks into one rate.</li>
  <li><strong>Ranch and lodge stays</strong> that include meals and some skiing, often with transport to a nearby ski area on set days.</li>
  <li><strong>Packages</strong> from resorts or tour operators that combine lodging with lift tickets, and sometimes rentals, lessons, or transfers, but not meals.</li>
</ul>
<p>The word "all-inclusive" is a marketing term, not a standard. Two packages with the same label can cover very different things.</p>

<h2>What's typically included, and what isn't</h2>
<p>Inclusions vary by property and season, but these are the line items to check:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Lift tickets:</strong> often included, but sometimes for fewer days than your stay, or only on certain days of the week.</li>
  <li><strong>Lessons:</strong> group lessons are sometimes included; private lessons almost always cost extra.</li>
  <li><strong>Rentals:</strong> frequently excluded, even at fully inclusive resorts. This is one of the most common surprises.</li>
  <li><strong>Meals:</strong> may mean every meal, breakfast and dinner only, or a packed lunch on ski days.</li>
  <li><strong>Drinks:</strong> some include an open bar or house wine; premium bottles and spirits are often extra.</li>
  <li><strong>Kids' clubs and childcare:</strong> clubs for older kids may be included, while care for babies and toddlers often costs extra.</li>
  <li><strong>Transfers:</strong> airport and mountain shuttles are sometimes included, sometimes optional add-ons.</li>
  <li><strong>Taxes, service charges, and spa:</strong> usually extra.</li>
</ul>

<h2>All-inclusive and package-style options in North America</h2>

<h3>Club Med Québec Charlevoix (Quebec)</h3>
<p>Club Med's ski-in/ski-out resort sits at Le Massif de Charlevoix, northeast of Québec City. According to Club Med, its ski stays include meals and drinks, group ski and snowboard lessons, and a lift pass. A 6-day lift pass is included with a classic seven-day package. Rental equipment has been reported as an extra cost, so confirm it when you book. Club Med had announced a second North American ski resort at Snowbasin in Utah, but that project was put on hold in 2023.</p>

<h3>The Ranch at Rock Creek (Montana)</h3>
<p>This all-inclusive luxury ranch near Philipsburg offers downhill skiing at Discovery Ski Area, about 35 minutes away, as a full-day included activity in winter. The ranch says it provides equipment fittings, ground transportation, a ski concierge, lift tickets, and lunch at Discovery. Ask how many ski days your stay includes and whether lessons are part of it.</p>

<h3>Triple Creek Ranch (Montana)</h3>
<p>Triple Creek Ranch in the Bitterroot Valley says its all-inclusive stay covers lift tickets, ski or snowboard rentals, and shuttles to and from Lost Trail Powder Mountain. Lost Trail doesn't run every day, so check which days you can ski during your stay.</p>

<h3>Alta Lodge (Utah)</h3>
<p>Alta Lodge is a lodge-style option rather than a full all-inclusive. Its winter rates include breakfast and a four-course dinner, plus afternoon tea. Lift tickets, lessons, and rentals are booked separately.</p>

