<h2>Pros and cons vs. booking separately</h2>
<h3>Pros</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Predictable budget.</strong> Most of the cost is paid upfront, so there are fewer surprise bills on the mountain.</li>
  <li><strong>Less planning.</strong> No separate lift ticket, lesson, and restaurant bookings.</li>
  <li><strong>Easy for groups.</strong> Nobody has to split dinner checks or track who paid for what.</li>
  <li><strong>Built for beginners.</strong> Included group lessons make it simple to try skiing without extra decisions.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Cons</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Less flexibility.</strong> You're tied to one property's restaurants and often one ski area.</li>
  <li><strong>Paying for what you don't use.</strong> If you skip lessons, eat light, or don't drink, a bundle can cost more than booking separately.</li>
  <li><strong>Limited choice.</strong> There are few true all-inclusive options in North America, so you can't pick from every mountain.</li>
  <li><strong>Fewer ski days than nights.</strong> Lift tickets may cover only part of your stay.</li>
</ul>
<p>To compare fairly, price the same trip à la carte. The <%= link_to "free Ski Trip Planner", ski_trip_planner_path %> has a budget worksheet for this, and <%= link_to "how to plan a ski trip", guide_path("how-to-plan-a-ski-trip") %> walks through each booking step.</p>

<h2>Who all-inclusive suits</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Families</strong> who want kids' clubs, lessons, and meals handled in one place. See our <%= link_to "family ski trip guide", guide_path("family-ski-trip") %> for more on picking a resort.</li>
  <li><strong>Beginners</strong> who will use the included group lessons every day.</li>
  <li><strong>Groups and mixed-ability parties</strong> where some people ski hard and others want spa time or snowshoeing.</li>
  <li><strong>Travelers who'd rather not plan</strong> and value one booking over the lowest possible price.</li>
</ul>
<p>Experienced skiers who want to chase powder at several mountains, or travelers on a tight budget, usually do better booking on their own. Our <%= link_to "cheap ski trip guide", guide_path("cheap-ski-trip") %> covers that route.</p>

<h2>How to find all-inclusive and package deals</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Resort packages:</strong> many ski resorts sell lodging-and-lift bundles on their own sites, sometimes with rentals or lessons.</li>
  <li><strong>Tour operators and ski travel agencies:</strong> companies such as Ski.com build packages that combine lodging, lift tickets, and other extras.</li>
  <li><strong>Ski-week deals:</strong> look for multi-night packages in early season or January, outside holiday weeks.</li>
  <li><strong>Ranch and lodge stays:</strong> check winter rate pages for what "all-inclusive" covers at that property.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Questions to ask before booking</h2>
<ol>
  <li>How many lift-ticket days are included, and on which days can I ski?</li>
  <li>Are group lessons included every day, and for which ages and levels?</li>
  <li>Are rentals included, and do they cover helmets?</li>
  <li>Which meals are included, and are drinks included or extra?</li>
  <li>Are kids' clubs or childcare included for my children's ages?</li>
  <li>Are airport transfers and mountain shuttles included?</li>
  <li>What taxes, service charges, or resort fees are added?</li>
  <li>What's the cancellation policy if snow is poor or the ski area closes?</li>
</ol>

